WEBVTT

1
00:00:12.261 --> 00:00:14.582
always worry about what their children are thinking.

2
00:00:15.843 --> 00:00:19.584
Sometimes, they imagine traveling through the minds of their sleeping children.

3
00:00:20.625 --> 00:00:23.386
They like to tidy up their children's thoughts for the next morning.

4
00:00:24.687 --> 00:00:26.527
It is like straightening up a chest of drawers.

5
00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:31.441
A mother folds her child's naughtiness into a small place in the back of the drawer.

6
00:00:32.521 --> 00:00:37.083
The better thoughts are kept in the front of the drawer, ready to be brought out the following

7
00:00:37.123 --> 00:00:39.403
morning. When Mrs.

8
00:00:39.463 --> 00:00:44.685
Darling traveled through her children's minds, she found the words "Peter" and "Neverland."

9
00:00:46.005 --> 00:00:50.026
Though she couldn't quite place the names, they seemed strangely familiar to her.

10
00:00:51.127 --> 00:00:53.547
Mrs. Darling thought they might be from her own childhood.

11
00:00:54.988 --> 00:00:59.390
But now that she was a grown-up, it was hard to remember exactly what the words meant.

12
00:01:00.590 --> 00:01:04.292
She vaguely recalled that Peter was a little boy who lived with the fairies.

13
00:01:05.233 --> 00:01:08.615
He lived on an island called Neverland, the land of dreams.

14
00:01:10.216 --> 00:01:12.497
Every child dreams about adventures in Neverland.

15
00:01:13.578 --> 00:01:14.018
But Mrs.

16
00:01:14.058 --> 00:01:17.860
Darling, as an adult, knew that these adventures were never true.

17
00:01:18.841 --> 00:01:20.221
One morning, Mrs.

18
00:01:20.261 --> 00:01:24.444
Darling found some stray leaves on the nursery floor just inside the nursery window.

19
00:01:25.824 --> 00:01:27.546
"How did these leaves get here?"

20
00:01:28.186 --> 00:01:29.187
she asked the children.

21
00:01:29.207 --> 00:01:34.531
"Some nights, Peter comes to visit," Wendy replied.

22
00:01:34.551 --> 00:01:37.113
"Peter is very naughty.

23
00:01:37.854 --> 00:01:40.056
He never cleans up after himself.

24
00:01:41.056 --> 00:01:45.180
He probably dropped the leaves and forgot to pick them up before he left."

25
00:01:46.401 --> 00:01:46.881
When Mrs.

26
00:01:46.921 --> 00:01:51.405
Darling asked Wendy to tell her more about Peter, Wendy described his visits.

27
00:01:52.766 --> 00:01:55.188
"He comes in through the window of our nursery.

28
00:01:56.064 --> 00:02:00.226
He sits at the foot of my bed and sometimes plays his flute."

29
00:02:01.786 --> 00:02:06.408
Unfortunately, Wendy explained, she was always asleep during Peter's visits.

30
00:02:07.409 --> 00:02:09.109
She didn't know how she knew about them.

31
00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:12.791
She just knew that Peter had surely been there.

32
00:02:14.192 --> 00:02:17.053
Mrs. Darling thought Wendy was only describing her dreams.

33
00:02:17.973 --> 00:02:21.415
The nursery was on the third floor and a very long way from the ground.

34
00:02:22.275 --> 00:02:24.456
How could anyone possibly come through the window?

35
00:02:25.536 --> 00:02:26.897
This story couldn't be real.

36
00:02:28.258 --> 00:02:31.439
But the leaves that she found on the nursery floor were certainly real.

37
00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:33.961
Mrs. Darling looked at them carefully.

38
00:02:35.081 --> 00:02:38.763
They were unusual leaves, certainly not found anywhere in England.

39
00:02:39.804 --> 00:02:42.585
The veins of the leaves formed a fine skeleton of lines.

40
00:02:44.326 --> 00:02:46.327
The following evening was Nana's night off.

41
00:02:47.268 --> 00:02:49.949
While the dog went out on the town, Mrs.

42
00:02:49.989 --> 00:02:52.491
Darling bathed her children and tucked them into bed.

43
00:02:53.631 --> 00:02:58.194
Then she sang to Wendy, John, and Michael, until they each fell asleep.

44
00:02:59.755 --> 00:03:02.977
Mrs. Darling sat down by the fire in the nursery and did her sewing.

45
00:03:03.778 --> 00:03:05.679
She loved to watch the children while they slept.

46
00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:07.861
They looked so peaceful.

47
00:03:08.621 --> 00:03:10.562
She, too, soon fell asleep.

48
00:03:12.223 --> 00:03:13.644
As she slept, Mrs.

49
00:03:13.684 --> 00:03:15.426
Darling had a dream about Neverland.

50
00:03:16.426 --> 00:03:19.628
She dreamed that a strange boy had flown far away from his home.

51
00:03:20.789 --> 00:03:22.790
What a strange dream for a grown-up to have!

52
00:03:24.231 --> 00:03:27.198
All of a sudden, the nursery window blew open.

53
00:03:28.279 --> 00:03:33.283
A boy dressed in the same skeleton leaves flew in, lightly touching down on the floor.

54
00:03:34.363 --> 00:03:38.187
Following him was a strange light no bigger than someone's fist.

55
00:03:39.428 --> 00:03:42.110
The light darted around the room as if it were alive.

56
00:03:43.151 --> 00:03:45.352
It was light as dust—fairy dust.

57
00:03:46.333 --> 00:03:47.914
Mrs. Darling recognized the boy.

58
00:03:48.555 --> 00:03:49.536
He was Peter Pan.

59
00:03:50.657 --> 00:03:54.119
And he was just as young as he had been when she first saw him as a little

60
00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:56.741
girl. She remembered him now.

61
00:03:57.462 --> 00:04:00.124
His tiny teeth sparkled like small pearls.

62
00:04:01.065 --> 00:04:02.506
At the sight of him, Mrs.

63
00:04:02.546 --> 00:04:03.947
Darling let out a scream.

64
00:04:05.068 --> 00:04:09.872
Just then, Nana returned from her evening out and burst into the nursery, barking.

65
00:04:10.813 --> 00:04:14.776
She growled and sprang at Peter, who leapt lightly up to the window.

66
00:04:16.097 --> 00:04:19.880
But before he could escape, Nana grabbed Peter's shadow in her mouth.

67
00:04:20.921 --> 00:04:24.304
As Peter flew away, his shadow tore off from his body.

68
00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:27.862
Concerned for Peter, Mrs.

69
00:04:27.903 --> 00:04:30.506
Darling ran outside and down the street to look for him.

70
00:04:31.286 --> 00:04:34.090
But all she saw was a shooting star in the sky.
